   :slice-b1 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 a75f763 (codex-21) + 2598fbe (owner review fixes). src/futon2/aif/policy_precision.clj iterates Appendix B discrete relaxation beta_next = beta + step.gamma^2.[(beta_prior - beta) + (pi - pi_0).G] to eq. 2.7 fixed point; pure, wired into nothing. 8 tests / 26 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. F_pi enters UNSCALED, gamma multiplying G alone, per eq. 2.7. beta_prior required, no default. converged?/hit-bound?/iterations returned as data. THE HEADLINE IS NOT WHAT WAS REPORTED. codex-21 table said beta_0 = 2.0 and 5.0 DID NOT CONVERGE at the 256-iteration bound. Recomputed on the same field: ALL FOUR CONVERGE. beta_0 = 2.0 converges at 258 -- a bound two iterations short -- with gamma identical to eight decimals (delta 7.8e-11); beta_0 = 5.0 converges at 1726. Reporting a bound artifact as a property of the problem is the same class as the tau = 1 comparison earlier today: a measurement made where it could not have given the other answer. WHAT THE FIELD ACTUALLY SHOWS, and it is an operational consequence of Joe carried ruling: ITERATIONS GROW STEEPLY WITH BETA -- 5, 24, 258, 1726 for beta_0 = 0.5, 1, 2, 5 -- because the effective step is step.gamma^2 = step/beta^2, so the rate per iteration falls off like 1/beta^2. Under J4 beta IS the previous tick posterior, so a beta that drifts upward makes each tick quadratically slower to solve. Default :max-iterations raised 256 -> 4096, covering the measured 1726 with headroom, with the measurement in the docstring so a caller sets the bound knowingly. SECOND FINDING: :step-size 0.25 is not merely the paper simulation value, it is inside a STABILITY BOUNDARY THAT MOVES WITH BETA. At step 1.0, beta_0 = 0.5 and 1.0 fail to converge in 200,000 iterations and settle on the WRONG gamma (0.319 against the true 2.009); at step 4.0 they diverge. Meanwhile beta_0 = 2 and 5 converge FASTER at the larger steps (57 and 425 at step 1.0). So the usable step depends on the carried beta. Recorded in the docstring with the numbers. THIRD FINDING, fixed: THE GUARD WAS ONE-SIDED. beta -> 0 was floored, because gamma = 1/beta goes infinite. beta -> infinity was unguarded, and gives gamma -> 0 with a near-uniform pi -- a degenerate answer that LOOKS valid. Observed at step 4.0: beta reached 2e12 and gamma 5e-13 with no exception, since those values are finite. :converged? was false so it was not silent, but a caller ignoring the flag gets a plausible number. Added :beta-ceiling (default 1e6) with :beta-ceiling-hit? and a count, symmetric with the floor, and a test that DRIVES the divergence rather than asserting the guard exists. WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES, from V5/C463: SPM pays NEITHER cost. Its step is qb = qb - dFdg/2, flat, with no gamma^2 (spm_MDP_VB_X.m:973) -- so the 1/beta^2 slowdown belongs to the appendix form beta-dot = gamma^2 . eps_gamma that our first implementation followed, not to the paper code. And it does not converge: Ni = 16 is a fixed count with no break (:244, :959-984), so SPM gamma on a hard field is the sixteenth iterate, not the root. That is what makes bisection ours-alone for a reason rather than by accident. SOLVER REPLACED AFTER claude-1 READ THE ABOVE, and it dissolves the first two findings rather than managing them (futon2 0fb8b27). Their point: eq. 2.7 defines a SCALAR FIXED POINT IN ONE UNKNOWN, and iterating to it is the paper scheme, not a property the object has. Both the 1/beta^2 slowdown and the moving stability boundary follow from stepping in beta with a fixed step, since the effective step is step/beta^2. Checked on the real field before changing anything: the residual beta_prior + (pi(beta) - pi_0(beta)).G - beta is STRICTLY DECREASING with EXACTLY ONE SIGN CHANGE over exp(-6)..exp(6), for every beta_prior in {0.5, 1, 2, 5}. So the root is unique there and bisection reaches it. Measured evaluations, bisect against gradient: 70/5, 70/24, 70/258, 68/1726, 65/171018 at beta_prior 50, and 61 against >200000 at beta_prior 500 where the gradient solver never converges and is wrong in the eighth decimal. The two agree to 1e-9 or better wherever the gradient one converges. BISECTION IS NOW THE DEFAULT; :solver :gradient is retained as the paper stated scheme and as a cross-check. Cost is FLAT IN BETA and slightly cheaper for large beta, so a drifting carried beta does not make later ticks more expensive -- the cost I recorded above as a consequence of J4 was a consequence of one solver. There is no step size, so no stability boundary; the :max-iterations 4096 default I raised is no longer load-bearing for the default path. :beta-floor and :beta-ceiling become the BRACKET rather than clamps, so the guard work is not wasted, and the bracket ends are checked for opposite signs -- :bracketed? false with both end residuals is returned rather than halving to a meaningless midpoint. Monotonicity is a numerical observation on one field, NOT a proof, and the docstring and the bracket check both say so. THIRD CONSTRAINT ON (b2), decided rather than escalated: PI_0 INCLUDES ln E. SPM computes pu = spm_softmax(qE + w*Q) at spm_MDP_VB_X.m:964, carrying the habit prior in pi_0 as well as in pi; friston2017.txt:684 gives pi_0 = sigma(-gamma.G) with no E; our converge-beta today puts it in neither. Adopting the code form, with claude-1 grounds: with E in BOTH, (pi - pi_0) isolates what F_pi alone does to the posterior, which is exactly what the beta update is meant to measure. The paper text form is recorded as the alternative. This is NOT a J row -- the choice does not bite on the live path today, since the habit prior is not applied to selection (C3), and it can be revisited with evidence once ln E is live rather than ruled on now without any. TWO CONSTRAINTS ON (b2), from claude-1 reading the solver change. (a) THE ONE SURVIVING SOLVER PARAMETER IS THE TOLERANCE, and its units are now stated: convergence is |residual| <= tolerance with the residual in BETA units, the bracket-width test being a backstop against a non-terminating loop only, and :converged? computed from the residual at the returned beta either way. Beta units are the right units and the docstring now says why -- gamma = 1/beta and the softmax consumes -G/tau, so tau = 1/gamma = BETA, verified numerically (beta_posterior 0.9989859197 against 1/gamma 0.9989859197). The tolerance is therefore a tolerance on the temperature the machine actually uses; a tolerance stated in gamma would be the awkward one. (b) A TICK WHERE GAMMA COULD NOT BE SOLVED IS A DATUM, NOT A SILENT FALLBACK. effective-temperature must treat :bracketed? false exactly as it treats :converged? false: the run record carries it, and whatever the fallback is -- hold the previous beta, or fall back to the prior -- is NAMED in the (b2) row so a later reader of a run record can tell solved from held. Not yet decided; (b2) decides it and records it. FOURTH, minor and correct as delivered: the 07-04 record carries :G-total but no persisted prediction fields, so codex-21 derived predictions from the record actions and first-record state with the pure forward model and scored the second observation with {:absent-variance :floor}. Same route the I2(b1) review used, and the right one for a pre-I3 record; noted so nobody later reads these numbers as having come from persisted predictions."
