  {:id :RUN6 :class :RUN :status :done :owner :claude-20 :reviewed-by "claude-20" :reviewed-at "2026-09-01" :covers-key :none :supersedes :RUN1
   :evidence "futon2 7eaca86 (codex-21) + the pre-flight script r6_zero_post_preflight.clj. Options :step-mission-detail-portfolio? and :eval-invariant-fallback?, both default true, run_tick_once passes false. 35 tests across the two suites, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK."
   :review "I RAN THE ENUMERATION MYSELF THIS TIME rather than checking the pointers I was handed, which is what went wrong at R1. grep over war_machine.clj, full_loop_runner.clj and enact.clj: FOUR http/post in war_machine (:2179 mission-detail, now suppressed; :2222 scan-portfolio, not reached because generate-war-machine calls the 0-arity which passes false; :2264 judge, suppressed by RUN5; :4099 the /eval fallback, now suppressed), FOUR in full_loop_runner (:107, :413, :586, :601 -- not reachable from run_tick_once), NONE in enact.clj. codex-21 list matches mine and it found no fifth POST. I ALSO WIDENED THE AXIS, because HTTP was the axis I was GIVEN and effects are the axis that matters. git: every call is `log`, read-only, no commit/push/add anywhere. Subprocess: `bb build-invariant-state-projection.bb --wm-status` spawns for real; its only writes go to fs/create-temp-dir, so it is harmless. THE DELIVERED WHOLE-TICK TEST IS NOT WHOLE-TICK. It redefines wm/generate-war-machine with a stub that calls the two producers of interest, so the real generator -- about twenty scans, the real judge, the real invariant inventory -- never runs. It therefore cannot catch a site nobody thought of, which is the only kind that matters. Kept, because it pins the two options cheaply, but it does not carry the acceptance. SO I WROTE THE REAL ONE and ran it: r6_zero_post_preflight.clj intercepts http/post and spit, leaves http/get alone so the tick sees real data, and runs tick/run-tick-once end to end. RESULT: a real diagnostic tick completes in 34 seconds with ZERO POSTS ATTEMPTED. That is the acceptance met on the actual path rather than on a stub. AND IT FOUND A WRITE NOBODY ENUMERATED: a tick also writes data/wm-trace/.lane-futility-index.edn -- persistent state in our own repo, carried forward between ticks, absent from RUN1 fourteen-effect audit and from my own review of it. It is a file in our tree rather than a call to another service, so it is far lower risk, but a validation run mutates it and RUN2 must say so rather than discover it afterwards. TWO NUMBERS R2 NEEDS AND DID NOT HAVE: 34 seconds per tick, so about 11 minutes for 20 ticks; and three files written per tick -- the trace record, the run receipt, and the lane futility index."
