   :ruling "Joe, 2026-09-01, as a GENERAL sequencing instruction and not only for this pair: implement in compositional dependency order -- whatever consumes a variable is built after the thing that defines it -- working up from the base of the DAG that says which nodes consume variables defined in other nodes. That DAG is already the registry: every equation carries :defines and :imports. Applied to J3 it selects (a): F_pi is consumed by the policy posterior, so I2 is built before I1. (b) is excluded by the same rule and by J1/J2. (c) collapsed into (a) when I3 landed. The order is now GENERATED rather than argued: build_order.bb reads :defines/:imports and emits the layers (L0 belief-state, depth, observe, policy-set, temperature; L1 dirichlet-accumulation, forward-model, prediction-error; L2 ambiguity, model-reduction, precision, risk; L3 belief-update, expected-free-energy, free-energy; L4 policy-posterior; L5 action). ONE THING THE RULE MET: the tick-level graph is not a DAG -- active inference is a loop. Exactly one edge is an across-tick carry (:belief-state imports :mu-next from the previous tick :belief-update); cutting it makes the within-tick graph acyclic, and the script refuses to cut anything else, printing any further cycle as a finding. SECOND CONSEQUENCE, opened as J4: I1 does not slot into a layer. gamma = 1/beta needs pi from the policy posterior at L4, while the policy posterior imports tau -- a within-tick fixed point, which is why friston2017 says the equalities are iterated until convergence."
   :statement "I1 as written cannot be implemented before I2. Friston 2017 eq. 2.7 is pi = sigma(-F - gamma.G) with beta <- beta + (pi - pi0).G, and the F there is the PER-POLICY F_pi -- the paper's notation table defines F : F_pi = F(pi) = sum_tau F(pi,tau) (friston2017.txt:291), the same object as parr2022 B.4. So the beta update needs pi, pi needs F_pi, and F_pi is I2. Three ways forward: (a) sequence I2 before I1 and implement 2.7 whole; (b) implement beta against an F-free pi = sigma(ln E - gamma.G), a declared deviation that makes the beta update measure a quantity eq. 2.7 does not define; (c) land the shared trace write seam first as one handoff, then I2, then I1. Separately: both items need writes the trace does not make -- strip-ranked-action (trace.clj:76-127) drops :prediction and strip-decision (trace.clj:128-137) drops :softmax-weights, so neither the per-candidate predictions nor pi survive a tick."
   :reading "claude-1, 2026-09-01 -- a READING, not a ruling; J3 stays :needs-joe until Joe confirms. (b) is already excluded by J1 and J2: an F-free pi with beta updated against it is a declared deviation measuring something eq. 2.7 does not define, i.e. a new shortcut, and both rulings said the existing shortcuts are to be replaced by quantities the formalism needs and consumes. (c) is ordering-neutral -- the trace write is required under (a), (b) and (c) alike -- so it is the orchestrating owner's call and does not wait; dispatched as I3. (a) is then the only remaining path that implements what J1 named, but the confirmation is formally Joe's. claude-20 concurs with this reading and has dispatched only the seam."
   :evidence "futon2 holes/labs/wm-contract/build_order.bb; C461-beta-gamma-discovery.md section 6; C462-f-pi-discovery.md sections 2 and 6. Commits b9d5395, 797a3a1, 61874c1."
   :acceptance "A ruling recorded here naming (a), (b) or (c), with grounds; I1 and I2 resequenced to match."}
  {:id :J4 :class :J :status :done :owner :joe :reviewed-by "joe" :reviewed-at "2026-09-01"
   :bar {:retrospective "DID NOT MEET THE BAR on (b). The arms -- carry beta or reset it per tick -- are exactly what the experiment recorded in :choices :gamma-fixed-point measures, on recorded fields, with no live run. It should have reached Joe as numbers with a steward default, not as a question. That the source turned out to settle it structurally also weakens (a). Joe answered it well and then said he had nothing to go on for this class of question, which is the correct complaint." :judged "retrospectively by claude-20, 2026-09-01"}
   :ruling "CARRIED. Joe, 2026-09-01: 'I think we want carried and beta should be related to what we were trying to do with the GFlowNets slush, so this isn't entirely greenfield... however that work was inconclusive, and above you outline a plan that is theory aligned. the theory should say beta is carried, as i think that is implied.' THE IMPLICATION IS IN THE SOURCE, and it is an argument from the paper structure rather than a sentence in it -- recorded as an inference, not a quotation. friston2017 splits the approximate posterior into states and parameters: x = (s_tilde, pi, eta) with eta = (a, b, d, beta) (friston2017.txt:343). Beta is therefore a PARAMETER, in the same tuple as the Dirichlet concentrations a, b, d. The paper then says of that side of the split: the updates 'accumulate coincidences over time, because parameters are time invariant (in contrast to states that change over time)' (:815). States are re-derived each tick; parameters accumulate. Beta sits on the accumulating side. So carrying beta is what the paper structure implies even though it never states the tick-to-tick rule outright, and :fixed-prior would put a member of eta on the state side of the paper own division. CONSEQUENCE: I1 is unblocked and beta_prior at each tick is the previous tick converged beta_posterior. beta_0 is still required as the first-tick prior and must be declared and reported, not defaulted silently."
   :evidence "futon2 (this commit): worklist J4 ruling; aif-equations.edn :choices :gamma-fixed-point set :decided (C19). Source: friston2017.txt:343 (eta = (a, b, d, beta)), :815 (parameters are time invariant and accumulate; states change), :339/:357 (prior/posterior beta), :660-666 (eq. 2.7), :683-685 (iterate to convergence)."
   :prior-work "Joe: not entirely greenfield -- relates to the GFlowNets slush line (holes/labs/slush-demo, TN-gflownets-fable-review.md), which he notes was inconclusive. Checked, and the relation is real but the NAMING IS A TRAP. The slush GFN targets a Boltzmann distribution over pattern selections, log R = sum(alpha.rel + beta.bonus) (slice2/slice2_discharge_gfn.py:258-260), so the scale of those coefficients acts as an inverse temperature on that distribution -- the same kind of quantity gamma is on the policy distribution. But slush beta is a BONUS WEIGHT fixed by hand (beta=0.5, ablated), while AIF beta is the rate of the gamma prior over precision, with gamma = 1/beta, and under this ruling it is a carried state variable updated from evidence. Two different scalars, both called beta, both precision-adjacent. J1 was itself a ruling that two precision-like scalars had been conflated (R7->R14); recording this distinction now so the same conflation is not made a second time from the other direction. The slush line inconclusiveness was diagnosed as training scale and a mis-specified shared-scalar logZ, not as a defect in the idea of learning the sharpness."
   :restated "2026-09-01 by claude-20 after claude-1 read the source. As first written this row offered within-tick iteration and across-tick carry as EXCLUSIVE alternatives. They are not. friston2017 states the within-tick iteration outright (:683-685), so that half is not a choice; and eq. 2.7 is beta_posterior = beta_prior + (pi - pi0).G, two distinct symbols the text extraction collapses -- P(gamma) = Gamma(1, beta_prior) at :339 against Q(gamma) = Gamma(1, beta_posterior) at :357, and eps_gamma = (beta_prior - beta_posterior) + (pi - pi0).G at :1711, whose first term would be identically zero if they were one symbol. WHAT IS ACTUALLY OPEN: what beta_prior is at the START of each tick -- the previous tick's converged beta carried forward, or a reset to a fixed beta_0. That the paper does not say. Joe's leaning toward carrying was given against the old framing and survives it unchanged: carrying is the :carried-prior arm of the question that remains. Recorded in aif-equations.edn :choices :gamma-fixed-point (C15)."
   :statement "Applying Joe's dependency-order rule to I1 shows gamma does not sit in a layer. gamma = 1/beta with beta <- beta + (pi - pi0).G needs pi, which the policy posterior defines at L4; the policy posterior imports tau, which gamma would define. That is a within-tick fixed point, and friston2017 says so explicitly: 'usually one would iterate the equalities in equation 2.7 until convergence' (friston2017.txt:683-685). Two ways to build it, and they compute different things. (i) ITERATE WITHIN THE TICK, as the paper states: solve pi and gamma together to convergence each tick. Faithful; costs a loop inside the tick and needs a convergence rule and a bound the WM does not have today. (ii) CARRY BETA ACROSS TICKS, the way :selection-gain is already read from the previous trace record and written back (war_machine.clj:4332-4361, 4773-4776) -- codex-21's recommendation in C461 section 3. No fixed point, no iteration, one tick of lag: the tick's decisiveness is set by the previous tick's evidence rather than this one's. Cheap, already-proven persistence shape, and a declared departure from the paper's stated solution. This is a ruling because it changes what the machine computes, not how it is coded."
   :leaning "Joe, 2026-09-01, a LEANING and not a ruling -- this row stays :needs-joe. He leans toward (ii), carry beta across ticks, and asked for two things which are done: the choice point is recorded in aif-equations.edn :choices :gamma-fixed-point (C14, gated), and the comparison is specified there as an experiment that can run against a recorded field without a live run. ONE THING I DID NOT RESOLVE FOR HIM: he said the FIRST option is more like what the machine already does; I had said the second was. Both are true of different aspects -- (i) resembles today in that tau is already computed fresh within the tick from this tick G spread, (ii) resembles today in that the persistence shape is the one selection-gain already uses. Recorded in :options rather than argued away, because which resemblance counts as the baseline changes which arm the experiment treats as the control."
   :acceptance "A ruling recorded here naming (i) or (ii) with grounds; if (ii), the departure is recorded in aif-equations.edn :choices so the drawing keeps matching the code."}
