   :reviewer-should-check "(1) THE CLAIM TO ATTACK is 'the score IS the selection law', because everything else here is bookkeeping around it. Check that policy.clj:593 takes its argmax of `full-scores` and that policy.clj:668-670 normalises THAT SAME BINDING -- not a second call with the same arguments. If they were two calls the claim would be 'two expressions that agree', which is the thing U1 point 4 found had quietly stopped being true one seam over. Related: the refactor moved the score out of softmax-weights, so confirm (a) still replays at delta EXACTLY 0.0 on all 24 recorded ticks -- that is the check that says the extraction was exact and not merely close. (2) THAT THE OFF-BY-DEFAULT CHECK HAS TEETH. full-score-law-is-off-by-default-test must run its default on a field where the two laws genuinely disagree (M-0 vs M-1); a default test on a field where they agree cannot fail. (3) THE 3-OF-3 RESULT, which I did not predict and which is the most consequential number here: I first wrote the test asserting the flip moves nothing and it failed, so the literal came from the measurement rather than the measurement from the literal. Re-run the ns and check that the moved-list literal is compared with = (not merely counted), that the mover sets come from minimal-mover-sets -- the SAME function (b) uses, not a reimplementation -- and that rank 130 is read from the decision's own :selection-law :chosen-rank. A reviewer who thinks the S4 field is too small to state a prediction from (3 ticks) should say so; SPEC U10 states it as a rate to be broken, not as a law. (4) THAT THE TWO FAILURE MODES ARE REALLY DIFFERENT, and that the split is not an excuse for not failing closed. The config throw is at war_machine.clj:5311-5312, in the let, before the try at 5379; if it were inside the try it would fall to default-mode-select and the whole 'loud config error' claim would be false. The per-tick fallback, by contrast, must NOT throw -- check that a declining tick still returns