   :reviewer-should-check "(1) THE CLAIM TO ATTACK is that the fixture is a PIN and not a copy, because every number below it rests on that. Check that controller-forms (zaif_full_loop_test.clj:94-104) actually reads ../futon3c/src/futon3c/agents/zaif_controller.clj at test time rather than a vendored copy, and reproduce BOTH mutations: fixture 0.65 -> 0.5 must go red, and the SOURCE constant 0.65 -> 0.55 must go red. If only the first fails, the pin is one-directional and the row overclaims. Restore futon3c to clean afterwards -- I did, and git status on that file is empty, but check it. (2) THE TWO CORPUS FINDINGS, which are the most consequential numbers here and are NOT reproducible from futon2 alone: re-run u6_zaif_decision.clj in the futon3c JVM (the invocation is in its header comment) and check that U6-ZAIF-DECISION.txt comes back byte-identical, that ':act G-term non-zero on' really is 0 of 114, and that 'chosen by TIE-BREAK' really is 83. The gamma claim in particular is a claim about a PRODUCT with an absent factor, so confirm the fallback chain in act-value (zaif_controller.clj L82-L85) is the only path and that no other caller supplies :task-belief -- the hydrator is one caller, not necessarily the only one. (3) THAT THE PLANT IS FENCED. The whole R4/R8 layer is planted and the row lives or dies on saying so. Check that plant-per-arm-prediction is the ONLY place a zaif value becomes a WM observation (grep the ns for plant-base and plant-channel), and that plant-dependence-negative-control-test genuinely gets three different orderings rather than three labels for one. A reviewer who thinks the whole R4 plant makes the R5/R6/R16 numbers uninformative should say so -- I think the structure survives and the ordering does not, and C489 sections 4 and 5 are written to be attacked on exactly that line. (4) THAT R8's ORDERING IS DECLARED FORCED. :433-440 asserts that the F_pi ordering equals the G-term ordering, and the comment above it says this is arithmetic.